      <!-- INTRO -->
      <p><strong>Compressed air duster aerosols</strong> are pressurised canisters that discharge a high-velocity stream of liquefied gas propellant to dislodge dust, particulates, and debris from sensitive electronic components, optical lenses, and precision instruments. They do not contain compressed air in the conventional sense — the propellant is a liquefied fluorinated gas that vaporises rapidly upon discharge, producing the cleaning gas stream. As regulatory pressure from the Kigali Amendment and the EU F-Gas Regulation intensifies, the aerosol industry is completing a major propellant transition from high-GWP HFCs such as HFC-134a to low-GWP hydrofluoroolefin (HFO) alternatives, principally HFO-1234ze. For engineers, procurement managers, and electronics maintenance professionals, understanding this transition — and its implications for ESD safety, flammability classification, and handling protocols — is increasingly important to both compliance and equipment protection.</p>

      <!-- SECTION 1 -->
      <section id="section-1">
        <h2>How Compressed Air Duster Aerosols Work</h2>
        <p>Aerosol duster canisters operate on the same fundamental principle as other liquefied-gas aerosol products. The propellant is stored inside the can in a two-phase equilibrium: a liquid phase at the bottom and a vapour phase above it that maintains internal pressure. When the valve is actuated, the pressure differential forces propellant vapour — and occasionally atomised liquid — through the valve orifice and the extension tube at high velocity. This directed gas stream displaces loose contamination from surfaces without physical contact. The propellant itself does not react with the substrate; it simply provides the kinetic energy needed to lift and remove particulates. Understanding this mechanism also explains a key misuse risk: inverting the can during use allows liquid propellant to be expelled directly, which can cause thermal shock and surface residue on components.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Valve type:</strong> Continuous-spray tilt or vertical valve with stem orifice sized for controlled flow rate</li>
          <li><strong>Extension tube:</strong> Flexible plastic straw enabling precise directional delivery</li>
          <li><strong>Propellant phase behaviour:</strong> Liquefied gas vaporises upon pressure release, cooling the stream and producing rapid gas expansion</li>
          <li><strong>Spray distance:</strong> Manufacturers typically recommend a minimum 5–10 cm standoff from sensitive components to prevent liquid impingement and cold shock</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The spray rate, plume geometry, and thermal output of the gas stream are all governed by propellant vapour pressure at ambient temperature, the valve orifice diameter, and the nozzle insert design. This is why selecting the correct propellant — particularly during the current industry reformulation — has direct consequences for product performance, not just environmental compliance. As described in our broader overview of <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/industrial" target="_top">industrial aerosol products and their engineering requirements</a>, each application category places distinct demands on the propellant system.</p>
      </section>

