      <!-- SECTION 2 -->
      <section id="section-2">
        <h2>Propellant Comparison: HFC-134a vs HFO Alternatives</h2>
        <p>For over two decades, HFC-134a (1,1,1,2-tetrafluoroethane) was the dominant propellant in electronics duster aerosols. Its non-flammability, low toxicity, compatibility with common valve materials, and stable vapour pressure made it the preferred choice. However, with a <a href="https://webbook.nist.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">global warming potential (GWP) of 1,430 relative to CO₂ over 100 years (NIST WebBook thermodynamic data)</a>, it is classified as a high-GWP F-gas subject to phase-down under multiple regulatory regimes. The primary HFO alternatives now entering the duster market are HFO-1234ze(E) (trans-1,3,3,3-tetrafluoropropene) and HFO-1234yf (2,3,3,3-tetrafluoropropene), both with GWPs below 10.</p>

